**Backend stack:** the backend is taken completely from `123dars/NIDS` (Flask, SQLAlchemy, Flask-Login). It is **not** rewritten in another framework. The parts NIDS lacks (live attack graph, Precog forecast, containment, scripted replay, polling-friendly endpoints) are added as separate add-on files that register extra routes in the same Flask app. NIDS's Docker, PostgreSQL and live packet sniffer are **not used**: the database is switched to SQLite through NIDS's own `DATABASE_URL` setting (to be verified in the build guide) and the sniffer is replaced by replay. NIDS has no WebSocket, so both clients poll its REST endpoints every 1 to 2 seconds. The built web app is served by the NIDS Flask app itself, so one deployment serves everything. NIDS pins older libraries (Python 3.9 and pinned `scikit-learn`, `pandas`, `numpy`); the Precog repository needs Python 3.11, so its Markov table is exported once to a JSON file in its own environment and the backend only reads that file.

