### 6.2 Repository & Merge Workflow

The aim is that four people can build in parallel with the fewest possible merge conflicts and no one blocked waiting for another.

- **One GitHub repository**, with **one folder per role**, a shared `contracts/` folder, one shared `web/` folder and a `docs/` folder, so each person normally edits only their own folder.
- **One branch per person.** Nobody works directly on `main`.
- **Only the team lead merges.** When a member finishes a piece of work and commits it to their own branch, they notify the lead and ask for a merge. The lead merges into `main` through a Pull Request.
- **Enforced by GitHub, not by trust:** branch protection on `main` plus a `CODEOWNERS` file make the rule real — nobody except the lead can merge, even by accident.

```text
repository-root/
├── contracts/   shared: data-format schemas and sample JSON (changed only through the lead)
├── lead-core/   LEAD-CORE's NIDS-based backend, add-on routes, Precog and integration work
├── detect/      DETECT's work
├── graph/       GRAPH's non-web work (ingestion, simulation, attack graph)
├── viz/         VIZ's non-web work (containment and re-evaluation)
├── web/         the ONE shared web project, divided into one sub-folder per area:
│                  mobile app (GRAPH), 3D console, charts and push (VIZ),
│                  project setup files (LEAD-CORE)
└── docs/        project documents
```

Because the web project is a single project that three people contribute to, each person works only inside their own sub-folder of `web/`. The exact folder layout, and how the shared pieces connect without anyone waiting, are defined step by step in each role's build guide.

### 6.3 Contract-First Integration

To avoid interlinked waiting, the data passed between roles is agreed **first**, in writing, before anyone builds:

- The `contracts/` folder holds a formal **JSON Schema** for each data format passed between roles, plus **sample JSON files** that follow it.
- The contracts cover the **new** endpoints (graph, forecast, containment, replay) and the shapes the web apps read from NIDS's existing endpoints. NIDS has no automatic API documentation page, so the endpoint list is kept in the project documents, and sample JSON lets the mobile web app and 3D-console builders see exactly what the backend will send without waiting for it.
- Each role builds and tests against the sample data, and later swaps in the real data without changing their own code.

