## 7. Demo Plan (Replay Mode)

**This is the single most important scoping decision in this document, and it overrides any earlier plan.**

The original project concept assumed a **live sandbox**: real virtual machines, a real scripted attack chain (Nmap scan → Hydra brute force → lateral movement → simulated exfiltration) executed live against infrastructure the team controls. That plan was reconsidered and **replaced with Replay Mode**, because the team is building this entirely through AI-assisted development ("vibecoding") with no prior background in networking, Linux administration, or offensive-security tooling, and no access to strong dedicated hardware.

### 7.1 What Replay Mode Actually Is

Instead of live VMs and live attack execution, the system replays a **pre-recorded, scripted sequence of attack events** — built from the CICIDS2017 dataset and/or JSON logs formatted to resemble real Caldera/Atomic Red Team output — through the exact same live pipeline described in Section 5. Detection, graph construction, forecasting, alerting, and containment all happen live and in real time, reacting to this replayed feed exactly as they would to genuine live traffic.

**To an evaluator watching the demo, the experience is identical:** they see detection fire, the attack-path graph draw itself live, forecast cones appear, and a containment action visibly stop the simulated attack from progressing further — in real time, on stage.

### 7.2 Why This Is the Right Call, Not a Weaker One

The original spec itself flags that a flaky live-hacking demo in front of a panel is worse than a rehearsed, reliable run — and recommends always having a recorded backup ready regardless. Replay Mode simply builds that reliability in from the start, rather than treating it as a fallback. It removes:

- The risk of live-network failure on demo day (Wi-Fi, laptop updates, venue firewall restrictions)
- The need for the team to learn real offensive-security tooling under time pressure
- Almost all legal/ethical risk, since no scanning or exploitation tool is ever run against any live network, classmate's device, or infrastructure the team doesn't fully control

### 7.3 The Demo Script (The "Wow" Moment — Unchanged)

1. Play the scripted/replayed attack sequence up to the point where Node A is confirmed compromised.
2. The system immediately shows: **"Precog predicts Server B is the next target — 74% confidence,"** rendered as a glowing forecast cone on the 3D graph and pushed to the mobile app.
3. The analyst taps **Contain** on the mobile app.
4. The replay resumes — the scripted next event (an attempted move to Server B) is shown as blocked, and the dashboard displays the attack chain terminated early, with the Precog accuracy counter ticking up.
5. This is the moment that separates this project from "yet another intrusion detection dashboard": the system acts on a prediction, live, and the audience watches an attack get stopped before it happens rather than after.

A recorded video of a perfect run will also be prepared as a backup, per standard practice for any live demo.

### 7.4 Where the Demo Runs

- **On the laptop:** the whole project runs on localhost and is shown from there.
- **On a phone:** the whole app is hosted on one free backend host, opened on the phone over HTTPS and installed as an app.
- **If a teacher asks for an app file:** the finished web app is wrapped into an APK and installed on the phones. If a teacher asks to see it in Android Studio, it is run there on request.

