## 10. Evaluation Methodology

Security ML work is judged harshly on evaluation rigor, so this is decided upfront rather than retrofitted later:

- **Train/test split:** proper train/test split, ideally training on CICIDS2017 and testing on a held-out day or attack-type to demonstrate generalization.
- **Standard metrics reported:** precision, recall, F1, and — critically — **false-positive rate** (a security system that cries wolf constantly is considered a failure in this domain).
- **Class imbalance handled explicitly:** attack classes are a small minority of the dataset; this will be addressed with an approach such as SMOTE or class weighting, and stated explicitly in the paper.
- **Precog-specific metrics:** forecast precision@k, a calibration curve (confidence vs. actual outcome), and containment success rate under simulated replay — reported in addition to detection accuracy, which most papers in this space stop at.
- **Experiment logging from Week 2 onward:** every experiment (features used, model, hyperparameters, resulting precision/recall/F1/false-positive rate) logged in a simple spreadsheet or tracker from day one, to avoid a painful, error-prone retrofit before the paper deadline.

