## 11. Mobile Application

The mobile app is an installable web app (PWA) and is the primary Mobile Application Development (MAD) deliverable. It covers: login/onboarding (a mocked demo account is sufficient — evaluators care about the security concept, not enterprise SSO), a live threat-level home dashboard, a chronological alert feed with plain-English explanations, a simplified 2D attack-detail view, the Precog forecast view with a one-tap **Contain** button, and a history/reports view showing past forecast accuracy. Design language is dark, high-contrast, and SOC-style throughout.

### 11.1 Delivery Approaches

The plan is **web first**: creating a native mobile app is considered difficult, so a complete, working web version is built first, and only after it works fully does anything mobile-native follow.

| Approach | Description | When to Use |
|---|---|---|
| **Primary — Installable web app (PWA)** | One web project that contains both the 3D console and the mobile app, built into static files served by the NIDS Flask app, polling the backend every 1 to 2 seconds, with Firebase push notifications for high-severity alerts. Shown on localhost on a laptop; for a phone, the whole app is hosted on one free backend host, opened on the phone over HTTPS and installed via "Add to Home Screen" | Default for everything, including the phone demo |
| **If required — APK** | The finished web app is wrapped into an APK (not a hand-coded native Android app), sent to the phones and installed | Only if a teacher requires an app file, and only after the web version works fully |
| **If asked — Android Studio** | The project is run in Android Studio | Only if the guide or a teacher asks for it |

The app is offline-tolerant: if connectivity drops, it shows the last-known state clearly marked as stale rather than crashing or showing nothing.

