## 14. Risks & Mitigations

| Risk | Mitigation |
|---|---|
| A chosen repository is poorly maintained, undocumented, or breaks | Documented fallback for the two highest-risk repos (Neha's ML model → CICIDS2017 labels; Sagar's Precog repo → rule-based lookup table) |
| Backend/real-time pipeline underestimated (historically the most common failure point) | Owned by the team lead (Sagar); scoped as its own dedicated week (Week 4) |
| Scope creep ("wouldn't it be cool if...") | Scope frozen after Week 3; anything new past that point is written as "future work" in the paper, not built |
| Live demo failure on the day (Wi-Fi, laptop issues, venue restrictions) | Replay Mode removes live-network dependency entirely; a recorded backup run is also prepared |
| Guide may not have a security background to validate technical correctness | Team stays self-directed on correctness (evaluation metrics, threat-model realism) rather than relying on guide feedback for technical validation |
| Novel feature turns out not to be as novel as assumed | Literature search (Google Scholar, IEEE Xplore) specifically for the Precog mechanism before finalizing the paper's novelty claim |
| Merge conflicts or a broken `main` branch | Folder-per-role layout, branch per person, only the lead merges, GitHub branch protection (Section 6.2) |
| The phone can only install the web app from a hosted HTTPS address, and the backend must be reachable from the phone | The NIDS Flask app serves the built web app itself, so one free backend host over HTTPS covers both; localhost remains the primary demo |
| NIDS ships with a default admin login, a hardcoded secret key and hardcoded database passwords | Move them to environment variables and never commit real values |
| NIDS runs its detector in a 30-second batch loop, which is too slow for live updates | Replay writes events already labelled, or the loop is shortened (decided in the build guide) |
| NIDS pins older libraries and expects PostgreSQL | Verify SQLite through `DATABASE_URL` and the pinned libraries early (Week 1); separate Python environments where versions clash |
| Evaluators may expect a native Android app for the MAD subject | Web-first; APK wrapper and Android Studio run are available on request (Section 11) |

